# DTF tier manifest: conda-remote-shard-recovers  (artifact-keeper#4829)
# Profile-set: filesystem / single + upstreams=mock-conda-sharded (the stdlib
# Python 3.14 server of conda-sharded-remote-virtual, reused unchanged: its
# /main/ channel lists dtfsh-tampered at one address and serves other bytes
# there until `GET /__heal`).
#
# THE BUG (#4829, 1.11.0 to 1.11.4)
# A remote conda channel refuses an upstream CEP-16 shard whose bytes do not
# hash to its address (502, #4577), but the proxy cache has already stored
# those bytes under the shard's path, and it classifies
# `shards/<sha256>.msgpack.zst` as immutable. Once the upstream serves the
# right shard the registry keeps answering 502 without asking it again, for
# the remote and for every virtual over it, until the entry is evicted by
# hand.
#
# THE PRIMITIVES
#   GET /conda/{remote|virtual}/noarch/shards/{sha256}.msgpack.zst
#   mock: /__manifest (addresses), /__requests (upstream hits), /__heal
#   proxy_cache_artifacts rows and the filesystem proxy-cache body
#
# Oracle (discriminating, RELEASE_GATE=1): see oracle.sh's header.
#
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=false: one admin login then a dozen calls back to back.
# UPSTREAM_ALLOW_PRIVATE_IPS=true: the remote's upstream is the mock on a
# private address (the profile also sets it, scoped to 172.16.0.0/12).
PROFILES="storage.filesystem upstreams.mock-conda-sharded"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
UPSTREAM_ALLOW_PRIVATE_IPS="true"
